course: arts
lesson: 32
title: Lego builds: instructions vs your own ideas
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Building with bricks is two kinds of fun: following the booklet step by step to make the spaceship on the box, and inventing something nobody has ever built, like a castle with a pizza shop on the roof.
explain: The booklet is not cheating; it is like a recipe that teaches you tricks, and builders then use those tricks in their own wild ideas.
explain: Bricks also teach real building: a tower stacked straight up snaps, but bricks overlapped like a wall hold each other, and a long bridge sags in the middle unless you add triangles or an arch.
explain: Building is also about people: asking before you take apart someone's model, keeping tiny pieces away from toddlers, and tidying the floor so nobody steps on a brick in the dark.
explain: People say it their own way: "our kid" in Manchester means my brother or sister, "ken" and "aye" in Scotland mean you know and yes, "bare" in London means lots of, and "sus" means suspicious or odd.
fact: LEGO is a Danish company founded in 1932, and its name comes from the Danish "leg godt", meaning "play well".
fact: LEGO bricks are made in factories in several countries, including Denmark, Hungary, Mexico and China, so the brand is Danish even if your brick was made elsewhere.
fact: Since 1958 LEGO bricks have used the same stud-and-tube fit, so very old bricks still click onto new ones.
fact: Following instructions is a real skill, and builders learn tricks from sets that they later use in their own models.
fact: Bricks that overlap the joins, like a real brick wall, make a much stronger tower than bricks stacked straight up.
fact: Stepping on a brick hurts because all your weight presses on one tiny hard spot of the foot, which has lots of nerve endings.
fact: A missing piece rarely ruins a model: builders swap in another brick, and single pieces can be ordered.
fact: A model you design yourself is called a MOC, "my own creation", and it can be as silly or wild as you like.
fact: Small pieces are a choking danger for babies and toddlers, which is why boxes warn about children under three.
fact: Someone's finished model is their work and time, so you ask before taking it apart, even if the bricks are shared.
fact: LEGO Masters is a real TV show where adult teams compete at building, and some adults design sets as their job.
fact: A long bridge with no support sags in the middle; triangles, arches and thicker layers make it stiffer, as real engineers know.
exercise: Oliver's cousin says LEGO is from China because of the box. Which is true? | It is Danish, made in many countries | LEGO was invented in a toy factory in China back in 1932 | LEGO is an American company that started in New York | It is from America
exercise: What does the name LEGO come from? | Danish for "play well" | The English word "leg", because bricks stand on little legs | The name of a big Chinese city where the first bricks were made | An old Latin word that means "brick" or "building block"
exercise: Ethan's dad's 1980s bricks click onto his new set. Why? | LEGO has kept the same fit since 1958 | Old bricks shrink to fit new ones | It is a lucky accident | New bricks are made soft to fit anything
exercise: Zara's brother says building from instructions is "not proper building". What is fair? | It is a real skill too | He is right, following the booklet is a kind of cheating at Lego | Only builds made from your own ideas count as proper Lego building | Booklets are for babies
exercise: Chen Hao's straight-stacked tower snaps when lifted. What makes it stronger? | Overlapping the bricks | Adding many more bricks straight on top so it is heavier | Using only the biggest bricks in the box for the whole tower | Lifting it much faster so it has no time to bend and snap
exercise: Why does stepping on a brick hurt so much? | Your weight is on one tiny spot | Lego bricks are secretly made of a thin, hard kind of metal | The bricks have sharp edges that cut like little knives | Feet are weaker at night
exercise: Ishaan lost one blue piece. What is the best move? | Use another brick | Throw the whole set away, since it can never be finished now | Never build with that set again and keep it in the box for ever | Buy the entire set again just to get the one missing piece
exercise: Freya's castle has a pizza shop on the roof. What is true? | Your own creations can be as wild as you like | Castles must look realistic or they are wrong | Free builds must follow the box picture | Pizza shops are not allowed in Lego
exercise: What is a MOC? | My own creation, a model you designed yourself | A set made only by the company | A missing piece | A Lego TV show
exercise: Lucas's two-year-old sister grabs tiny pieces. What is right? | Keep tiny pieces away, as toddlers can choke | She is fine if she only looks | Give her the smallest pieces to play with | Toddlers know not to put things in their mouths
exercise: Why do Lego boxes warn about children under three? | Toddlers can choke on small pieces | Toddlers cannot read the instructions, so they get very frustrated | The bricks are far too heavy for small children to lift safely | Toddlers dislike Lego
exercise: Amara's brother pulls a wing off her plane. What should he have done? | Asked first | Taken the wing anyway, because all the bricks belong to everyone | Taken the whole plane at once so there was no point in arguing | Waited until she was asleep so she would not see him take it
exercise: Noah laughs that grown-ups play Lego on TV. Which is true? | Many adults build, some as a job | Only children are allowed to buy and use Lego bricks | The TV show is fake and the models are built by children | Only robots build on it
exercise: Kateryna's Lego bridge sags in the middle. What would help? | Triangles, an arch or a thicker beam | Make the bridge even longer so it reaches further past the chairs | Take out the middle bricks so the bridge is lighter in the middle | Make it longer
exercise: When was LEGO founded? | In 1932 | In 1990 | In 1800 | In 2010
exercise: What do real builders do to make walls strong? | Stagger the bricks so the joins do not line up | Stack every brick straight on top | Use only one row of bricks | Leave gaps between bricks
test: Your mate goes, "If it's made in Mexico, it's a Mexican company, innit." What is the mistake? | Firms use factories in many countries | He is right, the factory always shows where the company is from | Companies are never allowed to use factories in other countries | Mexico owns LEGO
test: What would happen if you built a tall tower of single bricks straight on top of each other? | It would likely snap at the joins | It would be the strongest kind of tower that you could build | It would bend over a little bit but it would never break | It would bend like rubber
test: A kid from Toronto says, "Why's a tiny brick hurt so much, eh?" ("eh" means right?). What is the answer? | All the weight goes into one small spot | Lego bricks have tiny sharp spikes hidden on the bottom of them | Bricks sold in Canada are made much harder because of the cold | Bricks are hot
test: In a cartoon, a family throws out a whole Lego set because one piece is lost. What would a builder do? | Use a stand-in brick | Agree that the whole set is ruined for ever and throw it out too | Buy three more of the same set so it never happens again | Build nothing at all until the lost piece finally turns up
test: Ishaan says his set is "waste only" now ("waste only" means totally useless in Indian English). What is true? | A missing part rarely ruins a model | One missing piece wrecks every build | Sets cannot be fixed at all | Lego does not sell single pieces
test: Freya's dad asks why a castle has a pizza shop. She says, "Aye, it's my castle, ken." Which is true? | Her build, her rules | Castles must always be built to be historically correct | Her dad is right, it is a mistake she should take apart | Your own creations must copy real buildings exactly
test: A London kid says "bare pieces in the box, why you crying" ("bare" means lots of). What is he missing? | The plane is her work | Shared bricks always belong to whoever is the oldest in the family | Crying is not allowed in the house over toys or games | Planes do not really need wings, so she should not be upset
test: Your Gen Alpha cousin calls Lego Masters "lowkey hilarious" ("lowkey" means kind of). Which is true? | It is a real building show | Adults are not allowed to build with Lego on television | The big builds on the show are really made by robots | It is a cartoon
test: Which is the best way to make a Lego bridge between two chairs stiffer? | Use triangles or an arch and make the beam thicker | Make it longer and thinner | Use only round bricks | Build it on the floor first and lift it fast
test: Your toddler brother is near a big build with tiny pieces. What do you do? | Move the tiny pieces away | Let him play with them, because he will just spit them out | Give him a few small pieces to keep him busy and quiet | Nothing at all, because toddlers are careful with small things
test: Your mum's old Lego is from 1985. Will it fit your new set? | Yes | No, the old bricks are a slightly different size from new ones | Only if you sand the old bricks down to fit the new ones | Only with glue
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a tower of bricks stacked in vertical alignment is prone to fracture. | The joins align, forming weak lines | The quantity of bricks employed in its construction is insufficient | Vertical towers are expressly prohibited by the manufacturer's instructions | The bricks are brittle metal
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the origin of the LEGO company? | A Danish company founded in 1932 whose name derives from "leg godt" | A Chinese company founded in the present century | An American company founded in 1958 | A company whose name derives from the English word "leg"
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning small construction pieces and young children. | They pose a choking hazard under three | Small pieces are recommended for infants in order to develop their grip | Toddlers reliably refrain from placing any objects within their mouths | Only large pieces are risky
exam: Ascertain the conduct most appropriate prior to dismantling a model constructed by another member of the household. | The seeking of that person's permission | The dismantling of the model without notice | The concealment of the model's components | A demand for payment
